1 The metadata unit in proposal one has a unit class tag not in the defined class list of two content classes plus three exemptions. When a scanner from an older version encounters this unit, it skips the payload because the class is unknown, making all file metadata records inaccessible. This contradicts the settled requirement that all unit classes must be explicitly defined. The implementer would be forced to redesign the metadata storage to use an existing valid class, but after format freezing, this is impossible without breaking compatibility.

2 Proposal two truncates the checkpoint number in the accounting key to 16 bits. After 65536 checkpoints, the truncated value wraps around, causing duplicate keys for entries from different checkpoints. This violates the requirement for unique key identification. The implementer would need to increase the checkpoint field size, but the frozen format prevents any changes to key encoding.

3 Proposal three leaves 116 bytes of field widths undefined in the superblock pointer structure. When a pool grows to 20 devices, the device table cannot store device paths correctly due to unspecified field sizes. This contradicts the frozen layer requiring fixed field widths. The implementer would have to choose specific widths, but after freezing, this is not allowed.

4 Proposal four adds no tag field to the index node header. When implementing a new anti-aging scheme requiring a tag, there is no reserved space in the header. This violates the frozen index node internal layout layer. The implementer would be forced to resize the header, breaking compatibility with existing systems.

5 Proposal one uses a single object id for 233 metadata records in a shared unit. When a scanner processes this unit, all records appear to belong to one object, violating object uniqueness. This contradicts the requirement that each object must have a unique identifier. The implementer would need to assign unique object ids per record, but after freezing, this is impossible.

6 Proposal three moves the device table to a separate unit but keeps a 59 byte pointer in the superblock. When the pool has seven or more devices on a 512 byte sector system, the device table unit's structure cannot be properly referenced due to undefined field widths. This contradicts the need for fixed superblock and block header formats. The implementer would have to redefine the device table structure, but frozen format prevents changes.
